\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For an integer $d\ge1$, let $\mathbb Z^d$ have nearest-neighbor edges and root $0$. Chronological loop erasure of a finite path successively deletes each loop as soon as it is completed. Let $\mu_d$ be the infinite loop-erased random-walk law from $0$, defined as the local weak limit of loop erasures of simple random walks stopped on first leaving successively larger boxes. For two independent paths $\gamma_1,\gamma_2$ with law $\mu_d$, let $|\gamma_1\cap\gamma_2|$ count distinct common edges.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For which dimensions $d$ does $\mu_d$ have exponential intersection tails: is there $0<\theta<1$ such that $\mathbb P_{\mu_d\otimes\mu_d}(|\gamma_1\cap\gamma_2|>n)\le\theta^n$ for every integer $n\ge1$? \href{https://arquivo.pt/noFrame/replay/20201231041548id_/http://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~itai/stflouraug24.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine whether infinite loop-erased random walks have exponentially small probabilities of sharing many edges.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 10000020 (AMR-099-0020). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Itai Benjamini, \emph{Coarse Geometry and Randomness}, Saint-Flour lecture notes, version dated 30 October 2013; original author manuscript preserved in Arquivo.pt capture of 31 December 2020, Definition 4.16 and Open Problem 4.22, pp. 35--36. \url{https://arquivo.pt/noFrame/replay/20201231041548id_/http://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~itai/stflouraug24.pdf}.
